# Pitfalls — Classes & Encapsulation

## ❌ Pitfall 1: Public Data Members — Exposing Internals

```cpp
// ❌ Anyone can set balance to -1000
class BankAccount {
public:
    double balance;   // no invariant protection!
};

account.balance = -1000;  // perfectly valid, completely wrong
```

Once data is public, you can never add validation without breaking all callers.

## ❌ Pitfall 2: Initialization Order Surprise

```cpp
class Danger {
    int size_;
    int* data_;     // declared AFTER size_
public:
    Danger(int n)
        : data_(new int[size_])  // ❌ size_ is not yet initialized!
        , size_(n)
    {}
};
```

Members initialize in **declaration order**. `size_` comes after `data_` in the declaration, so `data_` reads an uninitialized `size_`. Fix: declare `size_` before `data_`, or initialize in the right order.

## ❌ Pitfall 3: Returning Non-Const Reference to Private Data

```cpp
class Config {
    std::vector<std::string> items_;
public:
    std::vector<std::string>& items() { return items_; }  // ❌ breaks encapsulation
    // Caller can: config.items().clear() — invariant violated!
};

// ✅ Return const reference or a copy
const std::vector<std::string>& items() const { return items_; }
```

## ❌ Pitfall 4: Forgetting `const` on Accessors

```cpp
class Point {
    double x_, y_;
public:
    double x() { return x_; }   // ❌ not const — can't call on const Point
    double y() { return y_; }   // ❌ not const
};

void print(const Point& p) {
    std::cout << p.x();  // ❌ compile error: non-const method on const ref
}
```

Accessors that don't modify state must be `const`.

## ❌ Pitfall 5: Using `this->` Everywhere

Over-qualifying with `this->` is not idiomatic C++. Only needed when a local variable shadows a member (which is itself a sign of poor naming):

```cpp
// ❌ Noisy and redundant
this->balance_ = this->balance_ + this->interest_;

// ✅ Clean
balance_ += interest_;
```

## Prevention Checklist

- All data members should be `private` unless the class is a pure data holder (`struct`)
- Write the invariant as a comment above the private section
- Mark all non-mutating methods `const`
- Declare members in the order you want them initialized
- Use member initializer lists — prefer `std::move` for string/container parameters

